INT. OFFICE TOWER ELEVATOR - NIGHT

Brushed steel. A cracked mirror. Floor numbers glow red. Four people ride in uneasy silence.

JOCELYN MARBURY (40s, trench coat, briefcase) stands near the panel. NADINE FOSSE (30s, scrubs under a puffer jacket) leans on the back rail. TEODORO LAVIGNE (50s, suit rumpled, tie pulled loose) stares at his shoes. VICTOR SANTAMARIA (20s, delivery uniform, a pizza box on his palm) taps the wall in time to a song only he hears.

The elevator stops between floors 22 and 23. Lights flicker.

                    VICTOR
          Uh.

                    JOCELYN
          Don't.

                    VICTOR
          I didn't press anything.

                    NADINE
          Nobody did.

Teodoro looks up. Sweat beads at his temple. He loosens his tie further.

                    TEODORO
          Somebody call security.

                    JOCELYN
          No signal.

                    NADINE
          I have bars.

                    JOCELYN
          Then she's lying.

                    NADINE
          Excuse me?

                    TEODORO
          Easy.

Nadine holds up her phone. Three bars. She dials. Jocelyn lunges and swats it out of her hand; it skitters across the floor. Victor steps on it. Nobody breathes.

                    NADINE
          What is wrong with you?

                    JOCELYN
          The fewer calls, the better.

                    VICTOR
          For who?

Jocelyn doesn't answer. Teodoro slumps against the wall, a hand to his chest. The lights die. In the dark, the sound of a zipper. A click.

The lights return. Jocelyn holds a small black pistol. It points at Teodoro.

                    JOCELYN
          Twenty-second floor, Mr. Lavigne. You were supposed to get off.

                    TEODORO
          I was --

The elevator lurches, drops six inches, and catches. Victor yelps, the pizza box flipping into the air. Nadine grabs his sleeve. Jocelyn staggers, and Teodoro lunges, wrestling her wrist as the gun fires into the ceiling. Plaster rains down. The doors judder, grind, and slide open on a blank concrete wall, the shaft's inner face.

Everyone freezes. A pigeon feather drifts through the gap.
